% ============================================================================
% igneous-rock-type — the two broad types of igneous rock and what actually
% defines each (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% Every igneous rock forms from cooled magma, but WHERE it cools splits every
% one into exactly one of two broad types: intrusive rock solidifies below
% ground, extrusive rock solidifies at or above the surface. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? igneous_rock_type(intrusive, $D)
%       % solidifies_within_earth
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `igneous_rock_type(type, description)` carrying the source citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on an igneous-rock-related term that is not one of these two. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the type it
% names:
%
%     ? igneous_rock_type($T, erupted_onto_the_surface_or_into_the_atmosphere)
%       % extrusive
%
% The two types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type         description                                              quote (verbatim)
%     -----------  -------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     intrusive     solidifies_within_earth                                  "Intrusive igneous rocks solidify within Earth."
%     extrusive     erupted_onto_the_surface_or_into_the_atmosphere          "Extrusive igneous rocks are erupted onto the surface or into the atmosphere."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from the U.S. National Park Service's
% "Igneous Rocks" geology page, which opens by stating "there are two broad
% types of igneous rocks" and gives each its own standalone, parallel first
% sentence under its own subsection heading. WebFetch-verified across two
% passes before writing, the second pass pulling the full first paragraph
% under each heading to confirm the target sentence stands alone as a
% complete, grammatically independent statement, and a third pass
% confirming the page names no third igneous-rock category (intrusive and
% extrusive are presented as the complete, exhaustive two-way split).
% `trust authoritative` — NPS is a U.S. government (.gov) agency, the
% primary tier this stdlib already reserves for other government sources
% (e.g. NASA, USGS, NOAA, Cal OES).
%
% Unlike most sibling tables in this directory, intrusive/extrusive is a
% genuinely EXHAUSTIVE two-way split, not an arbitrary subset of a longer
% list — the source's own opening line states there are exactly two broad
% types. So honest abstention is instead demonstrated with `hypabyssal`: a
% real geological term for rock that cooled at a shallow, intermediate
% depth, but one this source's own two-category framework does not name or
% pin to a defining sentence, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than
% inventing a description or a third category. Picked using the mandatory
% full-tree-grep-before-scoping discipline — zero hits for
% "igneous_rock_type"/"intrusive"/"extrusive" before this file was written,
% after checking two not-yet-reviewed science tables this window
% (heat-transfer.adj, chemical-bonds.adj — both closed exhaustive
% classifications, non-extendable); distinct from the sibling
% `earth-science/rock-types.adj`, which names the three ROCK-CYCLE families
% (igneous/sedimentary/metamorphic) by formation mechanism, not this
% within-igneous split by cooling location. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table igneous_rock_type {
    columns type, description

    row (intrusive, solidifies_within_earth)
    row (extrusive, erupted_onto_the_surface_or_into_the_atmosphere)

    source "Intrusive igneous rocks solidify within Earth."
    locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/geology/igneous.htm"
    trust authoritative
}
